   "text": "I read the drawing as a cyclic lipopeptide: a 3-aminotetradecanoic acid residue closes the ring together with seven amino acids.\n\n**Lipid chain.** Counting the zigzag from the left end gives 11 carbons up to the carbon that bears the NH, then CH2, then C=O. That makes 14 carbons in total, so the lipid is a 3-aminotetradecanoic acid. Its NH is acylated by Thr. Its C=O is joined to the NH of the first Asn. No wedge is drawn at C3, so I gave it no stereo.\n\n**Ring sequence.** Going around from the lipid C=O, the residues are:\n- Asn (CH2C(=O)NH2 side chain)\n- Tyr (4-hydroxybenzyl side chain)\n- Asn (second one)\n- Pro (the Asn C=O attaches to the proline N)\n- Glu (CH2CH2COOH side chain)\n- Ser (CH2OH side chain)\n- Thr (CH(OH)CH3 side chain), whose C=O closes the ring onto the lipid NH.\n\n**Stereochemistry.** I checked the first Asn from its drawn bonds and found it is S, as in L-Asn. I assumed the other residues are also natural L-amino acids, including L-Thr (2S,3R). For these I did not check the wedges or hashes individually.\n\nSMILES: CCCCCCCCCCCC1CC(=O)N[C@@H](CC(N)=O)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc2ccc(O)cc2)C(=O)N[C@@H](CC(N)=O)C(=O)N2CCC[C@H]2C(=O)N[C@@H](CCC(=O)O)C(=O)N[C@@H](CO)C(=O)N[C@@H]([C@@H](C)O)C(=O)N1"
